// R11 residual left by the 2026-09-15 block-scoped release: an `arc` local
// declared inside a VALUE-position block (a block used as an expression) is
// not released at that block's exit. `emitValueInto` owns those blocks and
// keeps its own mark without emitting drops, because the block's value may be
// the very local, and whether releasing it after the conversion into the
// destination is balanced depends on whether borrowck marks it moved into
// the destination, which it does not (an `arc` place is never made dead,
// R10 by design). So `a` is boxed, `r` receives a clone of it, `r` is
// released at the end of the `while` body (block-scoped release), and `a`'s
// own reference is never released: one box leaks per iteration.
//
// This program could not be written before 2026-09-15: the typed form was
// refused (the typechecker typed every block as unit) and the untyped form
// emitted `int64_t r` and did not compile. Typing a block by its tail
// expression made it expressible, and the leaks README's rule is one fixture
// per measurable gap, so this pins it the day it became measurable.
//
// The `owned` form of the same program, `let owned s = { let owned t =
// make() \n t }`, is refused by R10 ("cannot bind the unresolved name 't'"),
// so `arc` is the only owning shape that reaches this gap today.
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it (arc is
// scalar-first-refused there, same as examples/arc.cell). Lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level corpus loops (see
// examples/leaks/README.md).

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        let arc r = {
            let arc a = "leaked-value-block"
            a
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
